update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 180°C/350°F/gas 4.
2. Lightly spray a 12-hole muffin tin with cooking oil spray or alternatively you can line them with paper liners.
3. Pulse the cauliflower in a blender in two batches for about 30-50 seconds until a fine ‘rice’ type texture is formed - it’s ok if there are a few bigger pieces in there. (Be careful not to over process or the cauliflower will form a raw purée.) Measure out 480 grams of the cauliflower rice into a microwave safe bowl and heat for about eight minutes or until soft (alternatively, lightly steam over a pot of boiling water or in a vegetable steamer until soft).
4. Remove and allow to cool for a good five minutes before handling.
5. Using paper towels, an old tea towel or a cheesecloth, squeeze out as much liquid as you can until hardly any liquid is produced (It’s less messy if you wrap the cauliflower in the towel or cloth and squeeze it into a ball over the sink).
6. Transfer back into your bowl (make sure there’s no liquid in it), and add the whisked egg, cheeses, salt and garlic powder.
7. Divide the mixture into each muffin hole and firmly press them with your fingertips to create a ‘nest’ or cup.
8. Bake for about 15-20 minutes or until the cheese has melted, the cups are golden and the edges are browned.
9. Remove from the oven, break the eggs into each cup, season with salt and pepper, return to the oven and bake for a further 10-15 minutes, or until the whites are set and the yolks are cooked to your liking.
10. Allow them to cool for five minutes before handling them, or they may fall apart.
11. Lightly slide a knife around the sides of each cup, then using a fork, gently lift one side first (to make sure they’re not sticking to the bottom) and lift out of the pan.
12. Garnish with red chilli flakes and parsley if desired$r$ where name=$r$Cauliflower Egg Cups$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Start by draining the tofu from the water.
2. Heat a medium nonstick skillet (if you don’t have a nonstick skillet, make sure your pan is well-greased) over medium-high heat.
3. Add a tsp of non dairy butter, either way, for an even creamier taste.
4. Add tofu and all spices together.
5. Using a spatula, cut up the tofu in the pan to look like scrambled eggs.
6. Add the vegetable broth (1 tbsp at a time).
7. Bring the broth to a boil and allow each tbsp of water to fully absorb into the tofu before adding the next.
8. Remove from heat and season with sea salt and pepper, as desired.$r$ where name=$r$Tofu Scrambled Eggs$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 190°C/375°F/gas 5.
2. Coat six cups of a muffin tin with cooking spray or alternatively you can line them with paper liners.
3. Crack the eggs into a large bowl and whisk until smooth - this should only take a minute or less.
4. Add the spinach, bacon and cheese to the egg mixture and stir until all the ingredients are well combined.
5. Divide the egg mixture evenly between the six muffin cups.
6. Bake for 15-18 minutes or until eggs are set.
7. Serve immediately garnished with diced tomatoes and parsley if desired, or store in the refrigerator once cooled until ready to eat.$r$ where name=$r$Breakfast Egg Muffins$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 190°C/375°F/gas 5 and then lightly oil a 12 hole muffin tin.
2. Whisk the eggs, soured cream and milk in a bowl, then add the spring onions, chopped jalapeños, lime zest and sweetcorn, and mix.
3. Next add the polenta, flour, baking powder, salt and ¾ of the cheddar, and fold gently to combine.
4. Divide the mixture between the 12 muffin cups and top with a little more cheddar and a few more chopped jalapeños.
5. Bake for 12-15 minutes or until risen, lightly golden and cooked through.
6. While the muffins cook, pour the yogurt into a serving bowl and stir in the lime juice and zest.
7. Cool the muffins in the tin for 5 minutes before moving to a rack.
8. Serve with the lime yogurt on the side for dipping.$r$ where name=$r$Mexican Corn Muffins$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Finely chop the dates in a food processor until they form a ball of paste (add 1 tablespoon boiling water if they aren’t sticking).
2. Gently warm the nut butter and honey in a pan until it becomes a smooth, pourable liquid Stir all the remaining ingredients and the dates in a large bowl, then pour over the nut butter and honey mix.
3. Mix well until everything is evenly combined.
4. Line a 20 centimetre square tin or container with baking parchment, and pour in the mix., pressing down and into the sides with the back of a spoon.
5. Cover and chill for 30 minutes, before cutting evenly into bars.
6. Keep the bars in the fridge or an airtight container for 3 days, or in freezer if you want to keep them longer.$r$ where name=$r$No Bake Breakfast Bars$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Add all ingredients to your blender in the order listed and blitz until nice and smooth.$r$ where name=$r$Avocado Lime Smoothie$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 180°C/350°F/gas 4 and spraying a 12-cup muffin pan with non-stick cooking spray.
2. Meanwhile, beat the eggs, milk, ¼ tsp salt and ⅛ tsp black pepper together then stir in the cheese, spinach and roasted red pepper.
3. Divide this batter among the muffin-pan cups, top with prosciutto and bake for 20 to 25 minutes or until just set in the centre.
4. Remove from the oven and allow to cool on a rack for 5 minutes, then remove from cups.
5. Equally delicious served warm or cold.$r$ where name=$r$Spinach and Prosciutto Muffins$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Start by preheating the grill.
2. Heat the olive oil in a medium-large frying pan over medium heat.
3. Add half the asparagus and spring onion and cook for 2-3 mintues.
4. Meanwhile, whisk the egg and milk together and season with salt & pepper.
5. Pour half the egg over the asparagus and spring onions and cook for another 2-3 minutes.
6. Top the omellette with ricotta and cook under the grill for 2 minutes.
7. Remove from the pan and keep warm while you cook the next one.
8. Season with salt & cracked pepper and serve immediatley.$r$ where name=$r$Asparagus & Ricotta Omelette$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. 2 tsp olive oil Ina medium size bowl, add all the salsa ingredients, toss together 240g minced beef and set aside.
2. 2 tsp onion powder Next, bring a medium pan to medium-high heat.
3. Add the olive 2 tsp garlic powder oil, followed by minced beef.
4. Cook beef for 3-4 minutes or until 2 tsp ground cumin browned, then add all the spices and mix through to combine.
5. 2 tsp smoked paprika Cook for an additional 3-4 minutes or until beef is fully cooked.
6. For the salsa: Divide mixture on to four plates and top with salsa.
7. 140g cherry tomatoes, finely diced 2 red onion, finely diced avocado, chopped 4 tbsp fresh coriander, chopped 1 lemon, juiced salt & pepper, to taste$r$ where name=$r$Mexican Beef Breakfast$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Preheat oven to 160°C/140°F/Gas 3.
2. Put the tomatoes on a baking sheet with the oregano, drizzle over 1 tsp of olive oil, salt and cracked pepper, and roast for 1 hour.
3. Remove and set aside to cool alittle, Next, bring a fry pan to high heat.
4. Add the remaining olive oil.
5. Add the leek, garlic and chili flakes and fry for 6-8 minutes.
6. Meanwhile, prepare your toast on two plates.
7. Top with leeks and tomatoes.$r$ where name=$r$Chili Garlic Leeks & Tomato on Toast$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Add all ingredients to your blender and blitz until smooth and silky.$r$ where name=$r$The Greenest Smoothie$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. In a large non-stick pan, heat the oil over medium low heat.
2. Add the garlic and sun dried tomatoes and cook stirring for 1-3 minutes or until the garlic is fragrant and translucent.
3. Crack the eggs evenly around the pan and sprinkle with feta, salt, and pepper.
4. Cover and cook undisturbed until the eggs are cooked to your liking.
5. Jiggling the pan slightly will allow you to check the yolks - runny yolks will move, fully cooked yolks will be firm.
6. Transfer each egg along with some of the tomato and garlic, to a piece of toast and sprinkle with parsley if desired.$r$ where name=$r$Open Faced Breakfast Sandwich$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Preheat your oven or grill to a medium heat.
2. Slice the tomatoes in half horizontally.
3. Next, heat the oil in an 8-inch non stick pan over medium-high heat.
4. Once hot, add the garlic, stirring while it cooks for about one minute or until fragrant.
5. Add the tomato slices and basil.
6. Continue cooking until the tomatoes are becoming slightly blistered and soft.
7. While the tomatoes are frying, whisk the eggs, milk and a pinch of salt together in a bowl until well combined.
8. When the tomatoes are ready, transfer HALF of the tomato mixture onto a warmed plate.
9. Cover it and set aside.
10. Pour the egg mixture into the remaining tomatoes in the pan, stirring the mixture for a few seconds to combine the flavours.
11. Reduce heat to low-medium and add in the spinach leaves.
12. Arrange the mozzarella slices evenly over top, pressing them down slightly into the egg, and cook until the eggs are almost set (the centre should be slightly runny).
13. This should take around eight minutes.
14. Sprinkle the extra cheese (if using) over the top and transfer pan into the preheated oven.
15. Grill until the top is golden, puffed up and cooked through.
16. The edges and the bottom of the frittata should appear a light golden brown.
17. To serve, warm the remaining tomatoes and basil mixture, and spoon over the top.
18. Drizzle with the balsamic glaze.$r$ where name=$r$Caprese Frittata$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 230°C/450°F/gas 8.
2. Heat the olive oil in a large frying pan over a low heat, add the onions and a pinch of salt.
3. Have some extra water to hand.
4. Cook the onions, stirring, until they begin to brown.
5. Increase the heat to medium and continue caramelising the onions.
6. Add a few tbsps of water and scrape up the brown bits, incorporating them into the onions.
7. Allow the water to evaporate, then repeat this process until the onions have turned a dark brown colour.
8. Remove from the heat and set aside.
9. In the same pan cook the bacon over medium-low heat.
10. Once the fat has mostly rendered out, turn up the heat and brown the bacon until it’s brown and crunchy, then set aside on paper towels.
11. Remove most of the bacon fat from the pan leaving about two tbsps.
12. Add the sliced shiitake mushrooms and cook over medium heat until soft.$r$ where name=$r$SAVOURY BAKED OATMEAL WITH BACON, MUSHROOMS AND CARAMELISED ONIONS$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. mix the oats, chia seeds, almond milk, vanilla together in a medium sized bowl.
2. Stir through plums, cover and leave in the fridge overnight. give the oats a good stir, they should be creamy 2 bowls and top with the remaining plum, some or agave to sweeten.$r$ where name=$r$Plum and Almond Oats$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. 284ml pot buttermilk First, boil the kettle, and put the buttermilk and beaten egg in a egg, beaten, plus 2 poached eggs per person, to serve (optional) 00d processor.
2. 200g spinach Put the spinach in a colander and pour over boiling water to wilt it.
3. 175g buckwheat flour Squeeze out any excess water, add spinach to the processor and tsp gluten-free baking powder blitz to a smooth purée.
4. Pinch of paprika Next, put all the dry ingredients, plus 1 tsp of salt, in a bowl and Rapeseed oil, for frying gradually mix in the purée.
5. If it's a little too thick, add 1 tbsp water to loosen it into the consistency of batter.
6. Heat a drizzle of oil in a large, non-stick pan over a medium heat and spoon in two or three ladlefuls of batter.
7. Cook for 1-2 minutes or until bubbles appear, then flip over for 1 minute more or until cooked.
8. Repeat until you have 12 pancakes.
9. Serve topped with poached eggs, if you like.$r$ where name=$r$Spinach Protein Pancakes$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 180°C/350°F/gas 4.
2. Place potatoes and water in a microwave-safe dish.
3. Cover and microwave on high for 7 minutes or until tender (test with a fork) then drain well Spread potatoes in a 13 x 9 inch baking dish coated with cooking spray.
4. Top with the broccoli, onions and red pepper.
5. In a large bowl, whisk the remaining ingredients together until blended.
6. Pour over potatoes and broccoli.
7. Bake, uncovered, for 35-40 minutes or until centre is set.$r$ where name=$r$Potato Egg Bake$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 200°C/400°F/gas 6.
2. Meanwhile mix the rhubarb, 2 tbsp of the honey and the lemon juice in a baking dish in a single layer and cover tightly with foil.
3. Roast for 15-20 minutes until the rhubarb is very soft and allow to cool completely.
4. While it’s cooling, put the oats and hazelnuts in a roasting tray, mix the remaining 1 tbsp of honey with 1 tbsp of water, add to the nuts and toss to coat well.
5. Cook for 15-20 minutes until lightly browned.
6. Add the coconut chips and roast for a further 5 minutes.
7. In 4 small jars or glasses, layer the rhubarb and yoghurt, then top with the oats, nuts and coconut.
8. Serve immediately.$r$ where name=$r$Rhubarb Breakfast Pots$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Firstly, beat the eggs with 2 tbsp water and season.
2. Mix in the parsley, chives, garlic and parmesan.
3. Heat a 23cm heavy-based frying pan on a medium heat with a little oil.
4. Once the oil is hot, add one ladleful (about a third) of the egg mixture and tip the pan so it covers the base.
5. Reduce the heat and cook the omelette until it’s just firm, then flip it over and cook the other side.
6. Keep warm while you repeat to make three omelettes in total, adding a little extra oil to the pan each time.
7. Meanwhile, make the filling by combining the ricotta, basil, parmesan and some pepper.
8. Spread mixture over each omelette, roll up loosely like a cigar and cut into thickish slices.
9. Serve with a sprinkling of parsley.$r$ where name=$r$Frittatine with Ricotta$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by heating the olive oil in a large frying pan.
2. Once hot, sauté the onion and garlic for 5 minutes, or until the onion has softened and the garlic smells fragrant.
3. Stir in the harissa paste and ground coriander.
4. Let the spices roast in the pan for a few moments before adding the vegetable stock and chickpeas, including their liquid.
5. Cover the pan and leave to simmer for 10 minutes, by which point it should smell incredible.
6. Add the tinned tomatoes and courgettes to the pan and cook over a low heat for a further 10 minutes.
7. Once the tomatoes have broken down and the courgettes are tender fold in the baby spinach and fresh coriander and let the sauce gently bubble until it is rich and delicious.
8. Using the back of a spoon, make 4 hollows in the sauce.
9. Crack the eggs into the hollows, put a lid on the pan and let the eggs poach in the sauce.
10. Once the yolk has turned golden yellow and the white has set, remove from the heat and serve immediately.
11. Harissa is a North African and Middle Eastern condiment, most commonly found in Tunisia and Morocco, that is made from garlic, cumin, caraway, pounded chilli peppers, salt and a dash of olive oil.$r$ where name=$r$Spicy Moroccan Eggs$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Place egg whites in a small bowl, season with salt and pepper and whisk.
2. Heat the oil in a non-stick pan on a medium-high heat.
3. Add spinach and stir frequently, for 2 to 3 minutes, or until wilted and reduced in volume, add the corn, stir frequently, for 2 to 3 minutes until warmed through.
4. Set aside and keep warm Heat a separate pan over medium-low heat and add the egg whites.
5. Do not stir but as they begin to set, lift the edges letting uncooked whites slide below and cook When eggs are almost set, add spinach mixture and cheese.
6. Cook for a further 1 to 2 minutes, or until heated through.
7. Gently fold in half and serve$r$ where name=$r$Spinach and Gouda Omelette$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
