4. Cook the burgers for 5 minutes until the top side is slightly charred before flipping and continuing to cook for a further 5 minutes.
5. While the burgers are grilling, make the yoghurt sauce by combining all the ingredients in a small bowl before seasoning the sauce to perfection.
6. Serve the burgers with a dollop of yoghurt sauce on top for some added freshness.
7. These are great served with some cool fresh cucumber ribbons, chunks or slices.$r$ where name=$r$Greek Style Turkey Burgers$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Brush each aubergine slice with olive oil and cook in a hot griddle pan for 5-6 minutes on each side until really soft and starting to char, then grill the tomatoes for 4 minutes, cut-side down.
2. For the lentil mince heat the olive oil in a large pan over a medium- high heat.
3. Cook the onion until softened but not browned, add the garlic, cinnamon, paprika and oregano, and cook for a further minute, stirring constantly.
4. Add the chopped tomatoes, lentils, marmite and 50 millilitres of water, and allow to simmer for 10 minutes.
5. Spoon the lentil mince into bowls along with the aubergine slices and griddled tomato halves.
6. Top with the soya yogurt and garnish with the grated nutmeg a drizzle of extra-virgin olive oil and the flat-leaf parsley$r$ where name=$r$Vegan Moussaka Bowls (v)$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. In a pan cook the chopped onion and garlic in 1 teaspoon olive oil until softened.
2. Add the chicken and fry for a further 2 minutes, then stir in the tomatoes and stock before bringing to a simmer.
3. Add the cacao and chilli and cook for another 30 minutes, until thick and rich.
4. Stir in half the coriander and serve with fluffy rice, onion rings, lime wedges and a garnish of coriander.$r$ where name=$r$Chicken Mole$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Combine both soy sauces, brown sugar and water to a medium bowl, then decant half into another bowl and set aside.
2. Add the steak to the other half and marinade for at least 30 minutes or overnight.
3. Add the garlic to a large heavy pan and fry in the sesame oil for 1 minute.
4. Remove steak from marinade and sauté in the same pan for about 1-2 minutes on each side.
5. Next add the red pepper and sauté for a further 1 minute.
6. Pour in the reserved sauce and allow to simmer.
7. In a small bowl stir together the cornstarch and water.
8. Slowly add cornstarch mixture to pan, continuously stirring until sauce is thickened.
9. Serve immediately with green onions and sesame seeds for garnish.$r$ where name=$r$Sesame Beef$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Mix together the olive oil, balsamic vinegar, honey, garlic, salt & cracked pepper.
2. Submerge the chicken in the marinade, cover and place in the fridge for at least 1 hour, or you can leave it overnight.
3. Heat a barbeque or char-grill plate over high heat and cook the chicken for 3-4 minutes each side or until fully cooked through.
4. Make the dressing by mixing together the chillies, buttermilk, sour cream, lemon juice and cracked pepper.
5. Transfer chicken to a serving platter and serve with ranch dressing.$r$ where name=$r$Grilled Chicken Bites with Ranch Dressing$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Start by mixing all the sauce ingredients together.
2. Place the chicken wings in a large dish, pour the sauce all over, cover and leave in the fridge for at least four hours, or ideally overnight.
3. Preheat oven to 190°C/375°F/Gas 5.
4. Line a large ovenproof dish with baking paper.
5. Transfer the wings to the ovenproof dish, brushing the chicken wings with any extra sauce.
6. Place in the oven and cook for 1 hour or until wings are crispy.$r$ where name=$r$Gingery Chicken Wings$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Pound the steak with a rolling pin until it is about ½cm thick, then cut in half and set aside.
2. Next, mix the vinegar, thyme, grated garlic, half the oil and a couple of twists of black pepper in a bowl.
3. Place the steaks in the bowl, cover and set aside to marinate.
4. Fry the onions and sliced garlic in the remaining oil in a large non-stick frying pan.
5. Cook for 8-10 minutes, stirring frequently, until soft and starting to brown.
6. While the onions and garlic are cooking, steam, or boil the green beans until they are just tender.
7. Move the onions to one side of the pan.
8. Remove the steaks from the bowl, shake off any excess marinade, and sear them on both sides in the pan for 2½-3 mins, until cooked but still pink inside.
9. Pile the beans high onto plates, slice the steaks and place on top.
10. Add the wedges of beetroot, olives and remaining marinade to the pan and cook briefly to warm through, then spoon over and around the steaks.
11. Add the rocket and serve.$r$ where name=$r$Balsamic beef with beetroot & rocket$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Sauté the turkey over a medium-high heat until cooked through.
2. Add the onion and mushrooms and sauté for a further 5 minutes until tender and translucent.
3. Next add the tomato paste and water, stirring until the paste has dissolved.
4. Stir in the garlic, chili powder, paprika, cumin, vinegar, brown sugar, oregano, salt, and pepper and continue to cook for about 15 to 20 minutes, or until mixture has thickened.
5. Serve on your favourite buns (toasted if you like) with onions and coriander.
6. For extra flavour, you can melt a slice of cheese over the top!$r$ where name=$r$Sloppy Joes$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Coat a large pan with cooking oil spray.
2. Fry the onion and garlic over a medium heat until soft.
3. Add the carrot and mushrooms and fry gently until the mushrooms lose their water.
4. Add in the beef to the pan and cook until browned, add the tomatoes, tomato paste and water/stock.
5. Bring to a boil then reduce to a simmer for around 10-15 minutes until the liquid has reduced and thickened slightly.
6. Meanwhile cook the pasta as per the pack directions.
7. Stir the parsley into the sauce and serve over the pasta, topped with the grated parmesan.$r$ where name=$r$Spaghetti Bolognaise$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. In a heavy pan heat the oil and fry the onion for a few minutes until soft, add the garlic and paprika and cook for a further 1-2 minutes.
2. Add the pepper and mushrooms, and continue to fry for another 5 minutes or so until soft.
3. Add the vinegar, bring to a boil and reduce until most of the liquid has evaporated.
4. Pour over the stock and allow to bubble for a few minutes to thicken slightly.
5. Add the beef and cook for 2-3 minutes (depending on how rare you like it).
6. Stir in the fromage frais and season.
7. Serve on a bed rice or tagliatelle.$r$ where name=$r$Steak and Mushroom Stroganoff$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Cook the ground pork for 5-8 minutes in a non-stick pan, until cooked through.
2. Allow to rest on a paper towel lined plate and discard any juices that remain in the pan.
3. Add the vegetables to the same pan and cook for 5-8 minutes, until just tender then set aside.
4. Add the olive oil to the pan, and cook the lemongrass, garlic, ginger and shallots until softened.
5. Stir together the fish sauce and brown sugar, then add that to the pan.
6. Return the pork to the pan and toss to coat in the sauce before returning the veggies to the pan and stirring for a minute or two.
7. Serve hot over your favourite rice.$r$ where name=$r$Lemongrass Thai Ground Pork Stir Fry$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Stir the yogurt, onion, curry powder, lemon juice, half the oil, salt and cayenne, if using, together in a medium bowl.
2. Add the chicken and toss to coat evenly.
3. Cover and marinate in the refrigerator for at least 2 hours or preferably overnight.
4. Preheat your oven to 200C/180C Fan/Gas 6.
5. Line a rimmed baking sheet with foil and create a foil barrier to divide the baking sheet in half.
6. Place the Curried Chicken, in a single layer, on one side of the foil and the Chili-Lime Chicken, in a single layer, on the other side of the foil.
7. Roast for 15 to 18 minute or until the chicken is cooked through Whilst the chicken is roasting cook the rice and quinoa before adding to the bowls with the chicken, coriander and spring onions.$r$ where name=$r$Curried Chicken Bowls$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 180°C/350°F/gas 4 and line a 900g loaf tin with lightly oiled baking parchment.
2. Put the onion and aubergine in a large bowl along with the rice, tomato purée, bouillon, dill, spices and lamb.
3. Mix with your hands, add the egg and mix again.
4. Tip the tomatoes into the loaf tin then pack the lamb mixture on top, pressing down to compact it.
5. Cover the tin with foil and bake in the oven for 1 hour and 40 minutes, removing the foil for the last 15 minutes.
6. When the meatloaf has been in the oven for an hour, tip the peppers, courgettes and red onion into a roasting tin and toss with the oil.
7. Roast in the oven along with the meatloaf for 40 mins until the vegetables are tender and a little charred.
8. Remove both from the oven and let the loaf rest for 5 minutes, then tip out onto a serving plate.
9. Remove the baking parchment and scatter with dill.
10. Toss the roasted veg in the vinegar, garlic and mint and serve them with the warm meat loaf.$r$ where name=$r$Turkish Meatloaf$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Add the pepper garlic and ginger together in a food processor and pulse until minced, if you need to, scrape down the sides and pulse again.
2. Add the onion and pulse again until finely chopped but not puréed and watery.
3. Over a medium-high heat, heat the oil in a frying pan.
4. Add the onion mixture to the pan and cook, stirring occasionally, until softened, add coriander, cumin and turmeric and cook, stirring, for a further 2 minutes.
5. Meanwhile, pulse tomatoes in the food processor until fine then add to the pan along with the salt.
6. Reduce heat to a simmer and cook, stirring occasionally, for 4 minutes.
7. Add chickpeas and garam masala, reduce heat to a gentle simmer, cover and cook, stirring occasionally for a further 5 minutes.
8. Serve topped with cilantro, if desired.$r$ where name=$r$Chickpea Curry (v)$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Grate 100 grams of courgette.
2. Place the grated courgette in a tea towel or cloth, gather the edges and squeeze as much liquid as possible from the grated vegetable.
3. It's best to do this over the sink to avoid mess.
4. In a mixing bowl, add the squeezed courgette and all the other cake ingredients and mix well.
5. Spray a medium sized pan with non stick spray and heat to medium low.
6. Using a spoon, scoop up half of the mixture and add to one side of the frying pan.
7. Use your spoon to form a round flat cake, about ¥2 inch thick.
8. Then do the same with the rest of the mixture on the other side of the pan.
9. Let it cook for about 5-6 minutes or until the underside is a crispy golden brown.
10. Flip and cook the other side.
11. While the cakes are cooking, you can mix up your lemon dill yogurt sauce: Simply mix the yogurt, lemon juice, dill, and garlic salt in a small bowl and set it aside.
12. When the cakes are ready, place them on a plate and top with a dollop of the refreshing yoghurt sauce$r$ where name=$r$Courgette Tuna Cakes$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Season the onions with salt, then fry in the oil for 5-6 minutes over a medium-high heat stirring, until starting to soften and catching a little.
2. Reduce the heat and add the garlic, ginger and chilli.
3. Fry for a further 3 or 4 minutes, then set aside on a plate to cool.
4. Once cooled add the mixture to a food processor with the peanut butter, natural yogurt and soy sauce, then blend to form a coarse paste.
5. Put in a large mixing bowl, then coat the chicken in and coat thoroughly.
6. Leave to marinate for at least 30 minutes.
7. Pre heat your grill to high.
8. Thread the marinated chicken onto skewers and put on a foil-lined baking tray.
9. Grill for 8-10 minutes, without turning, until the chicken is cooked through and any marinade is golden brown.
10. Scatter with peanuts, coriander and spring onions and serve with lime wedges and steamed basmati rice.$r$ where name=$r$Chicken Satay$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 220°C/425°F/gas 7.
2. Then combine the maple syrup, soy sauce, sriracha and garlic in a small bowl, pour into a large sized resealable bag and add the salmon.
3. Let the fish marinate for 20 to 60 minutes, turning occasionally.
4. Meanwhile, lightly grease a baking sheet with non-stick spray.
5. Remove the fish from the marinade, pat lightly with kitchen towels and pour the marinade in a small saucepan.
6. Arrange the fish on the baking sheet and cook for around 8 to 10 minutes.
7. While the salmon cooks, bring the marinade to a gentle simmer over medium heat and reduce until it thickens into a glaze.
8. Spoon the glaze over the fish and serve.$r$ where name=$r$Maple SOY GLAZED SALMON$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 220°C/425°F/gas 7 and place a flat baking sheet in the oven to heat up.
2. Spread each pizza base with 2 tbsp of tomato sauce.
3. Carefully slide the topped bases onto the warmed baking sheet and cook according to the package instructions.
4. Five minutes before the end of the cooking time, scatter over the ham, dot the ricotta on top in blobs and place back into the oven to continue cooking.
5. Season and drizzle the pesto over each pizza before cutting into triangles and serving.$r$ where name=$r$Ham & ricotta pizzas with pesto$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Start by placing the beetroot, carrots, onion and chicken stock in a large pot and bring it to a boil.
2. Cover, reduce heat to a simmer and cook for 45 minutes - 1 hour, or until the vegetables are tender.
3. Add the vegetables and half the stock to a blender or food processor and blitz until smooth.
4. Next, stir in the honey, lemon juice, salt, and a little extra stock if it needs.
5. Refrigerate until cooled and serve cold if you want to try the traditional way.
6. Otherwise, it’s delicious served hot!$r$ where name=$r$Russian Borscht$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Begin by preheating your oven to 180°C/350°F/gas 4.
2. Meanwhile, place the chicken and corn starch in a sealable bag and shake well to evenly coat the chicken.
3. Heat the olive oil in a wok over a medium heat.
4. Add the chicken and stir-fry for around 2 minutes until it has turned golden brown before removing from the heat.
5. Once the chicken has cooked, drain the pineapple making sure to reserve some of the juice for the sauce.
6. To make the sauce, whisk together the pineapple juice, ketchup, rice vinegar, soy sauce, garlic and red pepper flakes in a small bowl.
7. Place the chicken pieces in a large roasting tin.
8. Scatter over the pineapple, onion and bell peppers before pouring over the sauce.
9. Remember to make sure that everything is well coated, as this will let the vegetables and chicken absorb the sweet and sour flavours.
10. Cover the roasting tin with foil and bake for 45 minutes, or until the chicken has cooked through and the sauce has become thick and sticky.
11. Scatter over a handful of spring onions before tucking in.$r$ where name=$r$Baked Sweet and Sour Chicken$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. Start by cooking quinoa according to packet instructions.
2. Meanwhile, take a medium sized frying pan and roast Brazil nuts for 4-5 minutes.
3. Make sure you’re constantly moving and shaking the nuts to make sure they don’t burn.
4. Prepare the broccoli by chopping the florets into very small pieces, or alternatively you can use a food processor or grater. (Because the broccoli is raw, you want very small pieces).
5. Next, prepare the dressing by adding all ingredients to a jar and giving it a vigorous shake.
6. Transfer cooked quinoa, broccoli and Brazil nuts to a large serving bowl.
7. Pour dressing all over, and toss really well to combine.$r$ where name=$r$Broccoli Salad$r$ and image_url not like $x$%abook-%$x$;
update public.recipes set method=$r$1. In a large heavy frying pan, cook the turkey along with the vegetables in sesame oil over medium-high heat.
2. Cook for 8 to 10 minutes until the turkey is cooked through and crumbled.
3. Stir through the hoisin sauce, vinegar, soy sauce, ginger and garlic.
